          Appropriations Committee Fiscal Summary

                                AB 1  (Aanestad)

Hearing Date:7/12/99            Amended:5/28/99        
Consultant: Karen French        Policy Vote:Education  12-0
____________________________________________________________ 

BILL SUMMARY: 

AB 1, an urgency measure, extends the repeal date (from  
July 1, 1999, to July 1, 2002) on a statute that allows  
Plumas County to receive small school and necessary small  
high school funding.

                         Fiscal Impact (in thousands)
 <u> 
Major Provisions </u>      <u> 1999-2000 </u>           <u> 2000-01 </u>             <u> 2001-02 </u> <u> Fund </u> 

Plumas USD           $660                    $660                $660General*

*Costs count toward meeting the Proposition 98 minimum guarantee.

STAFF COMMENTS:  

This bill meets the criteria for referral to the Suspense  
File.

Current law provides a minimum "floor" of funding for  
individual schools if those schools enroll fewer than  
specified numbers of pupils and are in school districts  
that enroll less than 2,501 pupils.  These "necessary small  
schools" and "necessary small high schools" receive  
specified allowances based upon the size of the school as  
measured by number of teachers and students, and in lieu of  
revenue limit funding that usually would be allocated per  
pupil in attendance.

Several school districts, with too many enrolled pupils  
otherwise to qualify, have been allowed statutorily to  
receive necessary small school allowances based upon local  
circumstances.  SB 1155 (Leslie) of 1997, authorized  
schools in the Plumas County Unfied District to be funded  
through necessary small school formulas by increasing the  
district-wide enrollment cutoff from 2,501 to 4,501 and  
specifying narrow criteria that only this district meets.   










The provisions of SB 1155 sunsetted July 1, 1999.


















































